{
  "summary": "This computer shows a serious warning sign: one program, svcupd.exe in your user AppData folder, is set to start itself in two separate ways and is also listening for network connections on a port that hacking tools commonly use. Together these match a common backdoor pattern, so look at this program first, using the fixes shown in the report. Separately, check that you recognize the extra <account-2> account on this computer.",
  "groups": [
    {
      "priority": 1,
      "title": "svcupd.exe starts itself several ways and listens on port 4444",
      "finding_ids": [
        "PERSIST-MULTI:e862e7c5",
        "WMI-CONSUMER:1dfdaadc",
        "PORT-ATTACK-TOOL:19e8a88a"
      ],
      "explanation": "All three findings point to the same program: c:\\users\\<user>\\appdata\\roaming\\svcupd\\svcupd.exe. It is set to start through a scheduled task named \\OneDriveSync, even though the task runs svcupd.exe rather than OneDrive. It is also set to start through a WMI event subscription called SvcUpd. WMI subscriptions that run programs are rare on personal computers and are hard to notice. On top of that, the program is listening on TCP port 4444 on all network addresses (0.0.0.0). That port is a common default for hacking tools and backdoors. The program sits in a folder your user account can write to, starts in more than one way so removing one can let another bring it back, and accepts network connections. That combination is a common backdoor pattern. Treat this as the top priority and follow the fixes shown in the report for each of the three findings. Deal with all of them together so no starting method is left behind."
    },
    {
      "priority": 2,
      "title": "Extra <account-2> account to confirm",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:fde0b291"
      ],
      "explanation": "There is a local account, <computer>\\<account-2>, with full control over the computer. Having a separate account like this is often good practice and may be perfectly normal. Because of the suspicious program above, it is worth confirming that you or someone you trust created it. If you don't recognize it, take it seriously and see the fix in the report."
    }
  ],
  "severity_notes": [
    {
      "finding_id": "PORT-ATTACK-TOOL:19e8a88a",
      "suggested_severity": "high",
      "reason": "The listening program is the same svcupd.exe from a user-writable folder that also has two separate ways of starting itself. It is open on all addresses, so in this context it is likely part of the backdoor pattern rather than a legitimate use of the port."
    },
    {
      "finding_id": "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:fde0b291",
      "suggested_severity": "medium",
      "reason": "On its own this would be low. Alongside strong signs of a backdoor on the same computer, an unexplained account with full control deserves a closer look."
    }
  ]
}
